hi there steve kaufman here  i haven't  done these in a while so i felt like  doing this evening  a question that i'm often asked is do i  worry or do i make mistakes or do i  confuse  languages that are similar mandarin and  cantonese italian spanish portuguese and  so forth  well the answer is that i probably do  the  weaker languages tend to get  interference from the stronger languages  so my cantonese will have mandarin in it  my portuguese will have spanish in it my  italian will have spanish in it  but  i don't worry about it  because i would rather be able to speak  three languages with mistakes  than speak one language with no mistakes  in any case i can't speak one language  with no mistakes so it really does  bother me knowing related languages  helps you to speak those languages even  with the odd bit of of mixture it's just  not a problem in my opinion  and you know i i'm going to say a little  bit more about this issue of of errors  uh  because i was you know i'm preparing uh  an ebook of my blog i've had my blog now  the linguist on language  for over five years and i've selected  some of my posts over these five years  and  put them into a book  and i've been working on it for the last  uh you know a little while  and  part of my blog posts or some of my blog  posts have been with reference to books  that i have read so therefore i've gone  back and reread some of these books or  at least leave through them  one of these books was a book called  second nature  brain science and human knowledge  by gerald edelman who is also a nobel  prize winner in medicine  and  i'm not sure i fully understand the book  gerald edelman has a background in  immunology and the  sort of selectionist response of our of  our immune system  uh to various uh you know viruses and so  forth and he takes that into his  analysis of the brain and how the  neurons  all however many billion of them there  are  in our  three pound brain how they  respond in a sort of selectionist way to  all of the tremendous  stimulus impulse experience information  that the brain gets deluged with  and he makes the point that in order to  cope with all this information the brain  deals with  comparisons  things that are similar metaphors he  says in other words it tries to look for  patterns  and in doing so it's able to cope with  all this information but by the same  token it's not able to be precise or  logical so i think that's a very  important point we have to accept error  as a part of of  dealing with this tremendous amount of  information and when we are dealing with  a language we're dealing with a  tremendous amount of information many  many words many combinations of words  and so  it is better for us to absorb all this  input the words the phrases look for  similarities look for patterns rather  than to try to come up with  precision error proof production of the  language so  you know just his analysis of how the  brain works kind of confirms  my approach to language my approach is  more many much varied rather than  precision  and  you know this evening uh you know i was  just thinking i went out for dinner  because my wife went off to toronto and  i didn't want to cook  so i took with me my book i'm reading a  portuguese a brazilian book  written in portuguese  i can't remember what it's called  actually but it takes place  it begins in  in sao paulo where the writer of the  book meets with this  older japanese lady who tells him a  story that took place in japan  so the bulk of the book so far takes  place in japan but i'm reading it in  portuguese  the book started  in brazil and the  first person in the book although i  don't think the author is but the first  person in the book is a brazilian of  japanese origin  so i'm reading this book  and as i read the book about japan  i have this tremendous longing to read  some japanese literature because there  is that certain  quality of quiet and stillness  which  may not be representative of life in  japan it is as it is today  but it is representative of some of the  most attractive aspects of japanese  culture  literature architecture  uh nature and so forth  so through the japanese book  my desire now to maybe the next book i  grab will be a japanese book so that  stimulated my interest  in the restaurant where i was sitting  it so happened that the owner is french  and  the next the  they share this patio with the japanese  restaurant  and i happen to know that the japanese  restaurant is run by a korean and his  partner  a korean canadian and his partner used  to be a japanese canadian  and  sitting there is this family that i take  to be some kind of middle eastern i  don't know if they're armenian or  lebanese or what they are persian maybe  and they're all eating with chopsticks  and they're eating japanese food  so all i'm saying is that there is so  much variety  in terms of human culture food language  experience  and the more of it we can experience  and take part in  the better and all of that is much more  important than  nailing down little bits of grammar  or how you're supposed to hold the  chopstick or whether you bow with your  left side of the head or the right side  all this other  detail  it doesn't really matter in my view  so really  today i didn't have much to say other  than we can't be  we can't be free of error we're going to  be  committing errors in our in our  discovery of languages  the brain  has us preconditioned to make mistakes  because our brain is looking for  similarities comparisons metaphors and  and in order to cope with this  tremendous amount of information there's  tremendous variety  the brain is structured in such a way as  to sacrifice accuracy  and that's good so we may as well  enjoy it i don't know if any of  all of this  makes sense uh i might add that the  weather here in vancouver has been  awesome the last little while  uh  the sun has been warm much warmer than  usual  and that i have been enjoying 